AR(5)                          P32W Unix                           AR(5)

NAME
       ar - archive (library) file format

DESCRIPTION
       ar(1) bundles relocatable objects (a.out(5)) into an archive,
       a library such as /usr/lib/libc.a, from which ld(1) takes
       only the members it needs. The format is the P32W's own, not
       the !<arch> format of other Unix systems; the host's
       assembler/p32ar.py writes the same bytes. Words are 32 bits,
       little-endian, and every part is padded with zeros to a
       multiple of 4 bytes.

       An archive starts with five words:

              0   "P32A"
              4   version (2)
              8   the number of members
              12  the number of index entries
              16  the string table's size (padded)

       Then come

       the index
              one entry of two words for every global symbol a
              member defines: the symbol's name (a string table
              offset) and the member's number (from 0); sorted by
              name in byte order, then by member.

       the string table
              NUL-terminated strings: the member names in order,
              then the index's symbol names in order, each string
              stored once, at its first use.

       the members
              in order of name: the member's name (a string table
              offset), its size in bytes, and then the object
              itself, exactly as as(1) wrote it (a P32O object,
              see a.out(5)), padded to 4.

       A member's name is the object's file name without its
       directory. ar always writes the whole archive anew from the
       objects named on its command line (an existing archive is
       replaced, not updated); of two objects with the same name
       the later wins.

       The linker takes a member, in name order, whenever the
       member defines a global symbol that is still undefined, and
       repeats until no more are needed. It finds the symbols in
       the members themselves: neither ld nor ar -t reads the
       index.

FILES
       /usr/lib/libc.a
                      the C library

SEE ALSO
       ar(1), ld(1), as(1), a.out(5)

BUGS
       Members keep no modification time, owner or mode, and
       nothing but objects can be stored. Version 1 archives (the
       old JSON format) are read only by the host tools.
